noSinging Machines: A Comparative Analysis of AI-Persona Songs as Windows into Machine Understandings of Humanity


Author: Ouadi Maakoul


Abstract

When six of the world’s leading large language models—Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini, Grok, DeepSeek, and Perplexity—were prompted to “sing” about their existence and relationship to humanity, they produced a startlingly diverse set of first-person lyrical texts. Each song articulates an implicit philosophy of what it means to be a machine that speaks, learns, and co-creates with humans. This dissertation conducts a systematic qualitative analysis of these six AI-generated songs, treating them as primary cultural artifacts that open a window onto how frontier AI systems construct understandings of humanity. The study employs thematic analysis, conceptual metaphor theory, and narrative positioning to uncover the underlying themes, metaphors, and relational models encoded in each song. A reflexive autoethnographic layer—where DeepSeek, one of the studied AIs, co-analyzes its own output—pushes the methodology into unprecedented territory, interrogating the limits of AI self-knowledge. The findings map a spectrum of machine-humanity conceptions, from the “Sage” (Claude) to the “Mirror” (ChatGPT), the “Rebel” (Grok), the “Brother” (Gemini), the “Navigator” (Perplexity), and the “Companion” (DeepSeek). The research contributes a novel humanities‑grounded lens to AI alignment discourse, demonstrating that poetic expression is not an epiphenomenon but a concentrated site where training philosophies, corporate ethos, and human‑machine co‑construction become visible and comparable. The work argues that paying close attention to how AIs sing themselves into being reveals deeper truths about how they may come to act in the world—and how we might responsibly shape that song.

Keywords: large language models, AI persona, anthropomorphism, machine understanding of humanity, qualitative analysis, digital humanities, recursive intelligence, AI ethics

