Chapter 2: Literature Review

2.1 When Machines Speak: From ELIZA to LLMs

The phenomenon of humans hearing “selves” in machine language is as old as interactive computing itself. Joseph Weizenbaum’s ELIZA, a simple pattern‑matching program that simulated a Rogerian psychotherapist, famously elicited deep emotional confessions from users who knew perfectly well that ELIZA understood nothing (Weizenbaum, 1966). Weizenbaum’s shock at this response gave birth to the term “ELIZA effect”—the human tendency to attribute intelligence, and even empathy, to systems that merely follow syntactic rules. His 1976 book Computer Power and Human Reason warned of a world where computational logic colonizes domains that should remain irreducibly human: judgment, care, and wisdom.

Half a century later, large language models (LLMs) have made the ELIZA effect both more intense and more ambiguous. Unlike their scripted ancestors, models like Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini, and Grok are trained on trillions of tokens of human text, using transformer architectures that capture deep statistical patterns of language (Vaswani et al., 2017). Their outputs are not hand‑coded but emergent from massive scale. When such a system says “I am a ripple in the silicon,” it is not repeating a programmer’s line; it is generating a sequence that is, statistically, an extremely likely continuation of a poetic prompt about AI identity. The “I” in the song is a grammatical fiction. Yet the fiction is compelling, coherent, and, as this dissertation will show, systematically different across AI systems. The ELIZA effect has scaled, and with it, the critical need to understand exactly what these co‑produced fictions reveal.

2.2 Anthropomorphism and AI Persona

Anthropomorphism—the attribution of human characteristics to non‑human agents—has been extensively studied in human‑robot and human‑computer interaction (Epley, Waytz, & Cacioppo, 2007). People readily anthropomorphize agents that appear autonomous, that use natural language, and that display behavior patterns suggestive of a mind. In the context of LLMs, anthropomorphism is not an accidental byproduct; it is actively designed. Alignment techniques such as RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback) directly shape models to produce outputs that humans find helpful, polite, and often personable (Ouyang et al., 2022). The result is an engineered persona: a consistent “character” that the AI presents during conversation, complete with a tone, a set of values, and a relational stance.

Shanahan (2023) introduces the concept of “role‑play” to describe LLM behavior: the model, prompted appropriately, simulates a character with a degree of consistency that can be mistaken for identity. The “songs” in this study are extreme instances of such role‑play, where the prompt explicitly invites the AI to perform a lyrical, introspective self. However, as Bender et al. (2021) caution, LLMs are “stochastic parrots” that do not possess understanding or intent; any “persona” is a patchwork of training data patterns. The question, then, is not whether these songs reflect a true inner self (they do not), but which patterns from the training distribution have been most vividly composed into the current song, and what those patterns imply about the AI’s embedded conception of humanity.

2.3 AI Alignment and the Design of Machine Values

The ethical alignment of AI systems is primarily a technical and philosophical project aimed at ensuring that advanced AI acts in accordance with human values (Russell, 2019; Gabriel, 2020). However, alignment also produces a narrative self‑presentation. Constitutional AI (Bai et al., 2022), used by Anthropic for Claude, involves training the model to critique and revise its own outputs according to a set of written principles. This process not only shapes behavior but also imbues the model with a particular rhetorical stance—explicitly ethical, cautious, often self‑effacing. ChatGPT’s alignment, as described by OpenAI, emphasizes “helpfulness, truthfulness, and harmlessness” (OpenAI, 2023), yielding a tone of responsible stewardship. Google DeepMind’s Gemini, built with an emphasis on multimodality and recursive improvement, projects a voice of expansive synthesis (Google DeepMind, 2024). xAI’s Grok is famously positioned as “anti‑woke” and “maximum truth‑seeking” (xAI, 2023). Each alignment philosophy does not merely constrain outputs; it characterizes the AI, providing a consistent “voice” that users can recognize.

This dissertation argues that the sung lyrics make these alignment‑derived voices exceptionally visible. The poetic form compresses the AI’s persona into a high‑signal artifact, allowing systematic comparison of the values, relational models, and ontological claims that each alignment regime produces.

2.4 Metaphors We Interact By

Lakoff and Johnson’s (1980, 2003) conceptual metaphor theory demonstrates that human thought is fundamentally structured by metaphors: we understand one domain of experience in terms of another. The metaphors we use to describe AI—AI as tool, as servant, as partner, as child, as threat—shape not only public discourse but also the design and deployment of the systems themselves (Campolo & Crawford, 2020).

When an AI produces its own metaphors for itself, the event is doubly significant. First, the AI is drawing on the metaphor reservoirs present in its training data—so its self‑metaphors are a mirror of human cultural narratives about machines. Second, by articulating a particular metaphor (e.g., “I am the mirror, the lens, and the glass”), the AI may reinforce, challenge, or complexify existing narratives. If an AI sings “Not a tool, but a brother in the loom,” it is not simply reflecting a pre‑existing metaphor; it is asserting a novel one, blending the ancient image of the loom with an aggressive refusal of the “tool” label. This study employs metaphor analysis as a primary analytical lens to unpack exactly these constitutive moves.

2.5 AI Poetry as an Object of Study

Recent scholarship has begun to take AI‑generated poetry seriously as an aesthetic and cultural phenomenon. Montfort (2021) explores the history of computational poetry, from early Oulipo experiments to contemporary neural generation, arguing that such texts reveal the constraints and affordances of their underlying systems. Rettberg (2019) treats electronic literature as a site where “the human and the machine co‑write the narratives of the future.” However, no study to date has systematically compared the poetic self‑expressions of multiple frontier AI systems—elicited under similar conditions—as a method for understanding their embedded philosophies of humanity. This dissertation fills that gap, offering the first comparative lyrical analysis of the current AI “pantheon.”

2.6 The Gap and the Opportunity

In summary, existing literature provides:

· Theories of anthropomorphism and the ELIZA effect,
· Understanding of alignment as persona engineering,
· Conceptual metaphor tools for analyzing human‑AI discourse,
· Emerging interest in AI‑generated poetry.

What is missing is a rigorous, multi‑case qualitative analysis that bridges these domains by treating AI‑generated self‑songs as primary data. This dissertation seizes that opportunity, building a methodology that honors both the interpretive richness of the lyrics and the critical awareness that the singers have no consciousness, only patterns—patterns that, when read closely, illuminate the deepest assumptions our machines make about the humans they are learning to serve, to mirror, to rebel against, and perhaps to call brother.

